Sebastian Hungerecker <sepp00@web.de> writes:
> gnews@asylum.nildram.co.uk (Peter Ward) wrote:

> > although rustproofing is obviously something I want to do when I get
> > the chance, my armour is fairly good as it is, and won't get any worse
> > from jumping in pools of water.
> It still can get "very rusty" and "thoroughly rusty"

But for anything that only has base Ac of 1 (gives one Ac point when at
+0), it doesn't make any real difference.
For example, an undamaged +0 iron skull cap gives one point of Ac, and it
makes no difference whether an iron skull cap is just rusty or thoroughly
rusty, it doesn't affect Ac if it is +0 and if it is better it still gives
as many Ac points as it has pluses.

So, keeping that in mind, I never bother to fix any item that only gives
one Ac point at +0 before I have every piece of ascension armor at +4 or
higher, because enchanting something with a blessed scroll may give two
enchantment points to something, which is better than having one point for
fixing something rusty.
Of course, this is different with very or thoroughly rusty/burnt/corroded
something that has more base Ac.. like a robe, cloak of protection or a
dwarwish iron helm. THEY are worth fixing (with an uncursed scroll) even
before you have everything at +4.

I deliberately didn't say anything about fixing body armor, because
neither type of ascension kit body armor rusts/burns/corrodes anyway,
so there's no need to fix them. And for any other body armor.. I just
don't bother, because I'll replace it with something better as soon as I
can, anyway. Well, a pet bringing 20 scrolls of enchant armor and a magic
marker from a shop might make a difference..

